TITLE:: FxUnit
summary:: what an effect is, whichever graph it ends up in
categories:: Libraries>ParaEQ
related:: Classes/ParaEQ, Classes/Compressor, Classes/FxChain, Classes/FxPresetStore, Classes/NodeProxy

DESCRIPTION::
The base of link::Classes/ParaEQ:: and link::Classes/Compressor::. An effect
here is two things that agree with each other: a piece of DSP written as a
pseudo-UGen that reads named controls, and an object that holds the settings and
pushes them at whatever node is running that DSP. The same DSP runs in three
places and the same object drives all three:

code::
// in a graph of your own, as a UGen; the object drives that graph's control
Ndef(\pad, { var sig = Saw.ar(110); ParaEQ.ar(sig, \eq) ! 2 });
e = ParaEQ(Ndef(\pad), \eq);

// on a bus, in place, with a node of its own
e = ParaEQ.onBus(16, 2);
e.enabled = true;

// in a chain of effects on a bus
FxChain.master.units[0];
::

subsection:: What enabled means

The same thing in every mode. With a node of its own, the node exists exactly
while the effect is on - built when it is switched on, faded out when it is
switched off, so off costs nothing. Driving a graph, a control called
code::name:: + code::On:: is set and the DSP crossfades to dry; the graph does
not know. In a chain, the chain places and drops the node - see
link::Classes/FxChain::.

subsection:: Driving a node

Anything that answers to code::set:: will do: an Ndef or any
link::Classes/NodeProxy::, a link::Classes/Synth::, a link::Classes/Group::.
Settings travel as one array on one control, so a push is atomic. A NodeProxy
keeps what it was set in its nodeMap, so a second object made for the same
proxy adopts the settings it already carries rather than resetting them, and a
rebuilt proxy comes back with them. With no target at all the object only holds
settings - the compressor keeps its sidechain eq that way and pushes it through
its own node.

subsection:: What a subclass provides

table::
## code::*ar:: || the DSP, reading link::Classes/NamedControl::s off a name
## code::*defName::, code::*synthDef:: || the in place synth for a channel count: In - *ar - XOut
## code::*controlName:: || the control read when none is named
## code::controls:: || the flat array the node is given
## code::settings::, code::settings_:: || what a preset and the clipboard hold
## code::asDict::, code::fromDict:: || what a set, a kit or a chain preset writes, enabled and all
## code::*guiClass::, code::*panelClass:: || its window, and the same as a view
## code::prAcceptControls:: || a node already carrying settings, read back
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
Drive a control on a node.

ARGUMENT:: target
An Ndef, a Synth, a Group - anything that answers to code::set:: - or nil.

ARGUMENT:: controlName
The control prefix on it; the subclass's link::#*controlName:: if left out.

METHOD:: onBus
A node of its own, in place on a bus. Nothing is built until it is switched on.

ARGUMENT:: bus
An index, a link::Classes/Bus::, or - with BusManager installed - a name.
ARGUMENT:: numChannels
ARGUMENT:: target
ARGUMENT:: addAction
Where the node goes in the tree; the tail of the default group by default.
ARGUMENT:: server

METHOD:: controlName
METHOD:: fade
METHOD:: ar
METHOD:: defName
METHOD:: synthDef
METHOD:: guiClass
METHOD:: panelClass
What a subclass provides - see above.

METHOD:: def
The in place def for a channel count, built once and kept.

METHOD:: prEnsureDef
Whether the def is on a server; if not it is sent, and code::done:: is called
once it has arrived. What keeps a node from being asked for before its def.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: enabled
On or off - see above. Setting it does whatever the mode calls for.

METHOD:: isActive
The same.

METHOD:: target
The node this drives, or nil. Setting one pushes the settings at it, or adopts
the ones it carries.

METHOD:: controlName
METHOD:: onName
The control prefix, and the on/off control's name.

METHOD:: isDriving
METHOD:: isOnBus
Which mode this is in.

METHOD:: synth
The node of its own, or nil.

METHOD:: server
METHOD:: bus
METHOD:: numChannels
Where a node of its own runs.

METHOD:: chain
The link::Classes/FxChain:: this belongs to, or nil.

METHOD:: label
What a window calls it.

METHOD:: action
A function called on every change, with the unit and what changed. The
compressor's sidechain eq reports through this.

METHOD:: analysisPlacement
METHOD:: analysisBusIndex
METHOD:: analysisChannels
METHOD:: analysisAvailable
Where an analyser reading this effect's output goes in the tree, as
code::[target, addAction]::, which bus it reads and how many channels: after a
node of its own; at the tail of a driven proxy's group, on the proxy's bus. A
driven Synth does not know its bus, so link::#-analysisBus:: has to be set by
hand before a spectrum can be drawn.

METHOD:: analysisBus
An override for the bus an analyser reads.

METHOD:: gui
Its window, made on the first ask and brought to front after that.

METHOD:: panel
The same controls as a view, for a window that shows several effects.

METHOD:: asDict
METHOD:: fromDict
code::(enabled:, settings:):: by default; a subclass may write another shape.

METHOD:: free
Drops the node, closes the window, leaves the chain.

EXAMPLES::

code::
s.boot;
Ndef(\pad, { var sig = Saw.ar([110, 110.7]) * 0.1; ParaEQ.ar(sig, \eq) }).play;
e = ParaEQ(Ndef(\pad), \eq);
e.isDriving;                 // true
e.enabled = false;           // Ndef(\pad).nodeMap[\eqOn] is 0 now
Ndef(\pad).nodeMap[\eq];     // the fifteen numbers
e.free;
Ndef(\pad).clear;
::
